Record Integrity {#chapter-integrity}
=================================

## Definition ## {#section-integrity-definition}

The <dfn>recordHash</dfn> is a Record-level <dfn>Integrity</dfn> value that covers the entire [=Record=] (its `did`, `metadata`, and `payload`) at the time the hash was calculated. It provides evidence that a [=Record=] has not been altered since that point, independent of whether the alteration occurred in `metadata` or in `payload`.

The [=recordHash=] is distinct from the [=Proof=] carried by an individual Version (see [Proof Procedure](#section-integrity-proof)). A [=Proof=] is an owner's cryptographic authorisation of one specific versions's content at finalisation. The [=recordHash=] is an unsigned, purely computational value; it does not require, and MUST NOT be interpreted as, an authorisation or attestation. It exists solely to make unauthorised or accidental modification of any part of the [=Record=] detectable.


## Record Hash Calculation ## {#section-integrity-recordHash}

The [=recordHash=] MUST be calculated as follows:

1. Serialise `did`, `metadata`, and `payload` (without `recordHash` itself) as canonical JSON ([[RFC8785]])
2. Calculate SHA-256 over the result

```
recordHash = SHA-256( canonicalize({did, metadata, paylaod}) )
```


## Version Hash Calculation ## {#section-integrity-versionHash}

The <dfn>Version</dfn> is represented by the <dfn>versionHash</dfn> and MUST be calculated as follows:

1. Serialise the version fields `state`, `parents`, `createdAt`, `modifiedAt`, `representations` and, where present, `issuance`, without `versionHash` and `proof` themselves, as canonical JSON ([[RFC8785]])
2. Calculate SHA-256 over the result

```
versionHash = SHA-256( canonicalize({state, parents, createdAt, modifiedAt, representations[, issuance]}) )
```


## Content Hash Calculation ## {#section-integrity-contentHash}

The <dfn>Content</dfn> is represented by the <dfn>contentHash</dfn> and MUST be calculated as follows:

1. Serialise the version fields `format`, `content`, `primary`, and `role` (without `contentHash` itself) as canonical JSON ([[RFC8785]])
2. Calculate SHA-256 over the result

```
contentHash = SHA-256( canonicalize({format, content, primary, role}) )
```


## Validation ## {#section-integrity-validation}

RWP <dfn>Validation</dfn> provides data structures and calculation rules that enable a [=Consumer=] to validate a RecordWeb object whenever the required information is available.

A [=Consumer=] MAY perform a Validation at any time and MAY repeat a Validation.

A [=Consumer=] MUST NOT represent the integrity of a RecordWeb object as successfully validated unless it has recalculated the applicable hash in accordance with this chapter and the calculated value equals the declared hash value.

A [=Consumer=] MAY retain, exchange, display, or discard a Validation result. RWP does not require a [=Consumer=] to retain a Validation result or to create a RecordWeb [=Record=] for that purpose.

Note: RWP does not require a Consumer to perform a Validation, does not prescribe when a Consumer performs a Validation, and does not prescribe the business, legal, or operational consequence of a Validation result.


### Validation Object ### {#subsection-integrity-validation-object}

A [=Validation=] applies to exactly one `validationObject` identified by a `did:rwp` identifier.

The type of the identified object determines the applicable integrity calculation:

- A [=Record=] identifier determines `recordHash` calculation.
- A [=Version=] identifier determines `versionHash` calculation.
- A [=Content=] identifier determines `contentHash` calculation.

A [=Validation=] of one object MUST NOT be represented as a [=Validation=] of any related [=Record=], [=Version=], or [=Content=] object unless those objects have been validated separately.

In particular, a [=Validation=] of a [=Record=] MUST NOT be represented as a [=Validation=] of all Versions of that [=Record=]. A [=Validation=] of a [=Version=] MUST NOT be represented as a [=Validation=] of all Representations or [=Content=] objects associated with that [=Version=].

A [=Version=] identifier MAY additionally be validated by verifying `proof` over `versionHash` against a verification method of the DID document, in accordance with [Cryptographic Procedures](#section-integrity-proof).


### Validation Result ### {#subsection-integrity-validation-result}

When a [=Consumer=] retains or exchanges the result of a [=Validation=], the result MUST contain:

- `validationObject`, containing the `did:rwp` identifier of the validated object;
- `validationType`, containing ether `integrity` or `signature`, depending on the kind of validation;
- `validationTimestamp`, containing the date and time at which the [=Consumer=] performed the [=Validation=]; and
- `validationResponse`, containing one of the [=Validation=] response codes defined in this Section.

The result is a statement by the [=Consumer=] that performed the [=Validation=] at the stated time. It is not a new state of the validated object and does not by itself establish the truth, authority, legal effect, or current availability of the validated object.

`signature` applies only to a [=Version=] identifier. Each result concerns exactly one validation object and one validation type. A `signature` result MUST NOT be represented as an `integrity` validation, and vice versa.

For `signature`, the verification method is taken from the DID Document of the record owner as resolved at `validationTimestamp`. Key state at the time of signing is not evaluated.

The following example records a successful [=Validation=] of one [=Version=]:

<pre class='example json' title='Validation result'>
{
  "validationObject": "did:rwp:d2d14675-50c9-463d-b564-1ea50644c836:efd09e7b-5f65-4637-b06c-404a9da97f0b:474425f9f1f2d1fe78bcec3f41a579c02d5261161c489ab22296980075f37895",
  "validationType": "signature",
  "validationTimestamp": "2026-09-16T12:55:00Z",
  "validationResponse": 200
}
</pre>

The following example records a [=Validation=] with a failed dependency:

<pre class='example json' title='Validation result with error'>
{
  "validationObject": "did:rwp:d2d14675-50c9-463d-b564-1ea50644c836:efd09e7b-5f65-4637-b06c-404a9da97f0b:474425f9f1f2d1fe78bcec3f41a579c02d5261161c489ab22296980075f37895",
  "validationType": "signature",
  "validationTimestamp": "2026-10-07T10:00:00Z",
  "validationResponse": 424,
  "dependency": "did:rwp:d2d14675-50c9-463d-b564-1ea50644c836:efd09e7b-5f65-4637-b06c-404a9da97f0b#key-2",
  "reason": "key-not-resolvable"
}
</pre>


### Validation Response Codes ### {#subsection-integrity-validation-responsecode}

The `validationResponse` property communicates the result of a [=Validation=] for the identified `validationObject`.

<table class="data">
  <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Name</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>200</code></td><td>OK</td><td>The applicable hash calculation was performed and the calculated hash equals the declared `recordHash`, `versionHash`, or `contentHash`.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>400</code></td><td>Bad Request</td><td>The `validationObject` or the [=Validation=] request is syntactically or semantically invalid.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>403</code></td><td>Forbidden</td><td>The identified object or information required to calculate its applicable hash exists or was identified, but the [=Consumer=] was not authorised to obtain it.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>404</code></td><td>Not Found</td><td>The identified object, or information required to calculate its applicable hash, could not be found or resolved. A system MAY use this outcome to conceal the existence of an object it does not permit the [=Consumer=] to access.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>406</code></td><td>Not Acceptable</td><td>The [=Consumer=] cannot process a required format, content type, representation, or other input required for the applicable calculation.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>410</code></td><td>Gone</td><td>The identified Version or its Content was deleted by a DeletionRecord. The applicable hash calculation is not possible.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>422</code></td><td>Unprocessable Content</td><td>The required input was available and the applicable hash calculation was performed, but the calculated hash does not equal the declared hash.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>424</code></td><td>Failed Dependency</td><td>The [=Validation=] could not be completed because a required dependent resource or information was unavailable, incomplete, or could not be validated. Where the cause is a denied authorisation, 403 applies instead.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

For results other than 200, two optional fields MAY be added:
- `dependency`: DID or absolute IRI of the resource that was unavailable (424), e.g. the verification method or the SchemaRecord.
- `reason`: short machine-readable text describing the cause, e.g. resolver-unreachable, key-not-resolvable, schema-not-found.

The codes defined in this section are [=Validation=] result codes. They do not define HTTP response semantics.

When [=Validation=] is exposed through an HTTP API, server-side failures MAY be communicated using applicable HTTP 5xx response codes. Such API failures are not [=Validation=] result codes defined by RWP.

A [=Consumer=] MUST NOT represent the result 403, 404, 406, 410, or 424 as evidence that the object is invalid or has been altered. Only 422 indicates that the applicable hash calculation was performed and the calculated hash does not equal the declared hash.


## Hashing Procedure ## {#section-integrity-hashing}

RWP uses exclusively **SHA-256** as the hashing procedure for:

- [=Version=] hashes (`versionHash`)
- [=Content=] hashes (`contentHash`)
- [=Record=] hashes (`recordHash`)
- Schema version hashes (`schemaVersion`)
- Parent references (`parents`)

A system MUST NOT use any hashing procedure other than SHA-256 for normative integrity proofs.

For all hash calculations over JSON objects, **RFC 8785 (JSON Canonicalization Scheme, JCS)** [[RFC8785]] MUST be used.

Requirements:

- Keys MUST be sorted alphabetically
- Unicode characters MUST be normalised (NFC)
- No superfluous whitespace or line breaks
- Numbers MUST be represented in their canonical form


## Proof Procedure ## {#section-integrity-proof}

If the schema prescribes `signaturePolicy` `owner` or `signaturePolicy` `system`, the finalisation act MUST attach at least one <dfn>Proof</dfn> to the [=Version=]. A [=Version=] without [=Proof=] is not finalized under such a policy.

A [=Proof=] MUST be a Data Integrity proof as defined by [[VC-DATA-INTEGRITY]] and MUST contain the following fields:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Field</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Value</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>type</td><td>MUST</td><td>`DataIntegrityProof`</td></tr>
    <tr><td>cryptosuite</td><td>MUST</td><td>`eddsa-jcs-2022` as defined by [[VC-DI-EDDSA]]</td></tr>
    <tr><td>created</td><td>MUST</td><td>ISO 8601 timestamp. SHOULD equal the `modifiedAt` of the Version.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>verificationMethod</td><td>MUST</td><td>DID URL of a verification method that is listed in verificationMethod of the DID document of the signing party and in the verification relationship required by proofPurpose</td></tr>
    <tr><td>proofPurpose</td><td>MUST</td><td>`assertionMethod` for Version proofs, capabilityInvocation for proofs of DID document versions</td></tr>
    <tr><td>proofValue</td><td>MUST</td><td>Multibase-encoded signature</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<pre class='example json' title='DID Proof'>
{ 
  "proof": [{
    "type": "DataIntegrityProof",
    "cryptosuite": "eddsa-jcs-2022",
    "created": "2026-10-26T18:20:00Z",
    "verificationMethod": "did:rwp:d2d14675-50c9-463d-b564-1ea50644c836:efd09e7b-5f65-4637-b06c-404a9da97f0b#key-1",
    "proofPurpose": "assertionMethod",
    "proofValue": "zPkfn4jxbW8fYn84s3hDZ73nafs9tMgti3hQ5vm4RFpsYbDjf1ZrnzosLt66TrVEmtS5yiZMacVwx4MiNjyigUM7"
  }]
}
</pre>

**Creation.** The unsecured document is the [=Version=] object including `versionHash` and excluding `proof`. The [=Proof=] MUST be created according to the algorithm of the cryptosuite `eddsa-jcs-2022`, using RFC 8785 JSON Canonicalization. The resulting [=Proof=] object is added to the `proof` array.

**Multiple proofs.** A [=Version=] MAY carry several [=Proofs=], for example from an institution and from a notary. Each [=Proof=] is calculated independently over the unsecured document, never over another [=Proof=]. Each [=Proof=] MUST be verifiable on its own.

**DID document versions.** The same procedure applies to the [=Proofs=] of DID document versions. A DID document version MUST carry at least one [=Proof=]. The unsecured document is the DID document version excluding `proof`.

**Verification.** A [=Verifier=] MUST recalculate the `versionHash` according to [Version Hash Calculation](#section-integrity-versionHash). It MUST then verify at least one [=Proof=] against the resolved verification method. A [=Verifier=] decides according to its own trust policy which verification methods it accepts. A successful [=Proof=] verification establishes that the holder of the verification method key signed this [=Version=]. It does not establish the authority of that holder. Evaluation of the key state at a given past time is outside the scope of this section.

**Key requirements.** The key MUST be an Ed25519 key. The key MUST be an Ed25519 key, expressed as Multikey with a publicKeyMultibase that starts with z and encodes the prefix 0xed01 followed by the 32-byte public key. The verification method MUST be published in verificationMethod of the DID document of the signing party and listed in the verification relationship required by the proofPurpose. Its controller MUST be the signing party. The signing party MUST be an [=AgentRecord=]; a verification method is not published in the DID documents of other [=Records=]. The key MUST be present in the current DID document version of the signing party at the time of signing. A key that is no longer present in the current version MUST NOT be used for new [=Proofs=].

NOTE: A successful Proof verification depends on the DID document of the signing party being obtained from the resolver responsible for its namespace, see [[#section-access-trust]]. Whether the signing party is an AgentRecord is determined by the `metadata.core.recordType` of its Record, not by its DID document. A verifier that is not authorised to retrieve this Record cannot establish this and obtains the validation response 403 or 424 ([[#subsection-integrity-validation-responsecode]]). This is not evidence that the Proof is invalid. Neither the registration of a namespace in the GNR nor the conformance of the signing party to the Root AgentRecord Schema establishes the authority of the signing party. This remains subject to the trust policy of the verifier.

